    {
      "title": "'Space, AI, other tech integration to shape India's geopolitical future'",
      "link": "https://www.business-standard.com/india-news/space-ai-other-tech-integration-to-shape-india-s-geopolitical-future-126082300308_1.html",
      "description": "Union minister Gajendra Singh Shekhawat on Sunday said the convergence of streams like space technology, AI, quantum technologies, robotics, semiconductors and high-performance computing will determine a nation's geopolitical positioning.\nSpeaking here on the 3rd National Space Day, the Union culture and tourism minister also said private participation in the space sector does not mean reducing the importance of entities like ISRO.\nPrivatisation of space is the \"democratisation of space opportunities,\" Shekhawat asserted at the event, organised by the Space Applications Centre (SAC), Indian Space Research Organisation (ISRO) and the Department of Science.\nToday is the age of space technology, artificial intelligence, quantum technologies, robotics, humanoids, advanced materials, semiconductors and high-performance computing, he said, adding that life will certainly be transformed in the coming years.\n\"Just as science is ultimately integrating in the form of a pyramid, in the coming",
      "source_name": "Business Standard Latest",
      "published": "2026-08-23T08:59:54"
    },
    {
      "title": "PM urges space startups to make India global hub for talent, innovation",
      "link": "https://www.business-standard.com/india-news/pm-urges-space-startups-to-make-india-global-hub-for-talent-innovation-126082300303_1.html",
      "description": "Prime Minister Narendra Modi has stressed creating an aura around the private sector in space research so that people worldwide feel that if they want to make a career, they should go to India.\nIn an interaction with CEOs and founders of 20 space startups on Friday, Modi also highlighted India's cost-competitiveness in launching space ventures.\nHe emphasised that the country's vast talent pool and high risk-taking capacity would allow India to rapidly establish a dominant position in the global space sector, according to the transcript of the interaction, which was released on Sunday.\nThe prime minister said the world must now be looking for Indian startups  wondering who is leading in this space.\nBecause whenever a breakthrough happens, people's attention shifts immediately, he said.\nOne of the CEOs told the prime minister that after India opened the space industry for private players, a very positive trend was observed and many Indian citizens living in the US and Europe now wan",
      "source_name": "Business Standard Latest",
      "published": "2026-08-23T08:51:46"
    }
  ]
